1
00:00:22,913 --> 00:00:26,553
[person 1] <i>Do you remember
where you were, Rhonda?</i>

2
00:00:29,893 --> 00:00:32,397
Do you remember how you got
to the TV studio?

3
00:00:33,366 --> 00:00:38,209
[person 2]
Did you recognize this person
or persons who took you?

4
00:00:39,178 --> 00:00:42,317
Did they say anything to you,
anything at all?

5
00:00:44,556 --> 00:00:45,659
No.

6
00:00:46,593 --> 00:00:50,601
Is that because
you can't remember
or don't want to?

7
00:00:54,341 --> 00:00:55,544
Yes.

8
00:00:58,416 --> 00:01:00,420
Can you remember anything?

9
00:01:04,461 --> 00:01:06,265
No detail is too small.

10
00:01:09,973 --> 00:01:11,510
[sniffles]

11
00:01:14,516 --> 00:01:15,818
Girlie smell.

12
00:01:20,393 --> 00:01:21,596
What was that?

13
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:25,036
Girlie smell.

14
00:01:25,236 --> 00:01:26,840
Girlie smell. That's good.

15
00:01:27,307 --> 00:01:28,511
That's really good, Rhonda.

16
00:01:29,112 --> 00:01:31,515
- What was...
What was the girlie smell?
- Girlie smell.

17
00:01:31,516 --> 00:01:35,290
Girlie smell.
Girlie smell. Girlie smell.

18
00:01:35,291 --> 00:01:38,129
- [crying]
Girlie smell. Girlie smell.
- Whatever she's saying

19
00:01:38,229 --> 00:01:39,733
- is clearly upsetting her.
- Girlie smell.

20
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:42,138
- Girlie smell. Girlie smell.
- We're done for today.

21
00:01:42,304 --> 00:01:44,743
Girlie smell. Girlie smell.

22
00:01:54,629 --> 00:01:58,469
[Bret]
<i>Though she returned to school,
Rhonda did not come back.</i>

23
00:01:58,470 --> 00:02:01,109
- [people clapping]
<i>- No one could console her,</i>

24
00:02:01,275 --> 00:02:02,912
<i>and no one could protect her.</i>

25
00:02:03,614 --> 00:02:05,919
<i>What she felt, she felt alone.</i>

26
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:09,593
<i>The world was lost to her
and her to it.</i>

27
00:02:10,528 --> 00:02:13,668
<i>Looking back-- even though
I didn't know it then--</i>

28
00:02:14,234 --> 00:02:16,472
<i>I deeply related
to this feeling.</i>

29
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:28,063
<i>I was convinced
of three things.</i>

30
00:02:28,262 --> 00:02:31,836
<i>One,
Rhonda's kidnapping was related
to Matt's disappearance.</i>

31
00:02:31,837 --> 00:02:36,513
<i>Two,
Robert Mallory was responsible
for both disturbing acts.</i>

32
00:02:36,947 --> 00:02:40,887
<i>And three, I was the only one
who could see it,</i>

33
00:02:40,888 --> 00:02:44,362
<i>and therefore the only one
who could stop him.</i>

34
00:02:45,130 --> 00:02:48,504
<i>Though it scared me,
I had to take action.</i>

35
00:02:48,971 --> 00:02:50,574
<i>No one else was.</i>

36
00:02:50,575 --> 00:02:52,443
<i>I deeply understood
this feeling...</i>

37
00:02:52,444 --> 00:02:55,017
- [doorbell rings]
<i>- ...as many young people do.</i>

38
00:02:55,584 --> 00:02:57,320
<i>Being alone in it.</i>

39
00:03:00,795 --> 00:03:01,897
Mr. Kellner?

40
00:03:02,698 --> 00:03:04,868
Yeah. Who are you?

41
00:03:04,869 --> 00:03:08,276
I'm Bret.
I'm a friend of your son.

42
00:03:09,278 --> 00:03:11,315
Can we talk? It's urgent.

43
00:03:22,270 --> 00:03:27,247
[Jon] So you were...
are close with Matt?

44
00:03:28,884 --> 00:03:30,253
No.

45
00:03:31,388 --> 00:03:34,294
Uh, not that close. Uh...

46
00:03:35,096 --> 00:03:36,533
but close.

47
00:03:38,871 --> 00:03:41,943
Yeah,
did I ever really know him? I--

48
00:03:43,514 --> 00:03:45,918
Uh, what I'm trying to say
is there's...

49
00:03:46,787 --> 00:03:48,725
there's closeness
and then there's proximity.

50
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:50,359
Why is it
that you're here, Bret?

51
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,531
I wanted to talk to you
about Matt.

52
00:03:52,532 --> 00:03:54,400
I'm convinced his disappearance
is connected to--

53
00:03:54,401 --> 00:03:57,609
I'm--
I'm sure you mean well, son.

54
00:03:58,476 --> 00:04:00,013
But I've already
talked to the police,

55
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:03,453
and they are of the opinion
that whatever happened to...

56
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:09,732
Whatever happened to Matt,
he did to himself.

57
00:04:10,801 --> 00:04:12,938
You know,
ran away or hurt himself.

58
00:04:12,939 --> 00:04:12,972
You know,
ran away or hurt himself.
He wouldn't have done that.

59
00:04:12,973 --> 00:04:14,609
He wouldn't have done that.

60
00:04:15,511 --> 00:04:19,719
He wasn't unhappy, sir.
He was very happy.

61
00:04:21,422 --> 00:04:22,826
Everyone who knew him.

62
00:04:23,293 --> 00:04:26,867
I mean, really knew him,
knows that.

63
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:29,471
[chuckles softly]

64
00:04:32,645 --> 00:04:35,016
You know,
this is the only house
he ever lived in.

65
00:04:37,822 --> 00:04:39,324
When he, um...

66
00:04:40,594 --> 00:04:42,899
When he hit
his teenage years, he, uh...

67
00:04:43,834 --> 00:04:47,407
he suddenly became removed.

68
00:04:48,476 --> 00:04:52,350
Like suddenly quiet,
suddenly secretive.

69
00:04:53,621 --> 00:04:55,224
And then at some point,

70
00:04:55,992 --> 00:04:58,764
he became like a boarder
who rented a room here.

71
00:04:58,765 --> 00:05:01,703
A stranger who occasionally
joined us for meals.

72
00:05:02,905 --> 00:05:08,784
I knew him as well as
any parent can know their kid.

73
00:05:11,623 --> 00:05:16,566
You know, I can trace the
constellation of the freckles
on his back by memory.

74
00:05:25,416 --> 00:05:27,020
But I didn't really know him.

75
00:05:27,454 --> 00:05:31,730
I didn't know
what kept him up at night.

76
00:05:32,732 --> 00:05:35,103
I didn't know
what he dreamt of.

77
00:05:36,673 --> 00:05:38,042
Who he loved.

78
00:05:39,646 --> 00:05:43,854
Did Matt ever talk to you
about a boy
named Robert Mallory?

79
00:05:44,321 --> 00:05:45,591
No.

80
00:05:46,125 --> 00:05:47,260
No, but I did meet him.

81
00:05:47,261 --> 00:05:48,997
You met Robert?

82
00:05:48,998 --> 00:05:50,366
- Yeah.
- When?

83
00:05:50,367 --> 00:05:53,038
Matt invited him over
for dinner a few weeks ago.

84
00:05:53,039 --> 00:05:54,676
My... [scoffs]

85
00:05:55,043 --> 00:05:57,881
wife made spaghetti and clams,
and I guess he must've liked it

86
00:05:57,882 --> 00:05:59,418
because he asked
for the recipe.

87
00:05:59,519 --> 00:06:01,054
Said he wanted
his own mother to make it.

88
00:06:01,055 --> 00:06:02,526
"His own mother?"

89
00:06:02,926 --> 00:06:04,761
[Jon]
Yeah, he was really very sweet.

90
00:06:04,762 --> 00:06:07,201
I never would have guessed
he was such a troubled kid.

91
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:08,637
What do you mean?

92
00:06:08,737 --> 00:06:11,710
When Matt disappeared,
I hired a private investigator.

93
00:06:11,810 --> 00:06:14,348
That's the reason
I know about Robert's issues.

94
00:06:14,782 --> 00:06:18,422
And my PI figured out
pretty quickly that Robert
was institutionalized,

95
00:06:18,423 --> 00:06:23,131
but the psychiatric evaluation
that was done
at the time is missing.

96
00:06:23,132 --> 00:06:24,434
Missing?

97
00:06:25,170 --> 00:06:26,607
Missing from where?

98
00:06:26,706 --> 00:06:31,817
Look, Robert's family,
they are very powerful, okay?

99
00:06:31,983 --> 00:06:34,120
Very well-connected, wealthy.

100
00:06:34,121 --> 00:06:37,127
They want a file to go missing,
it goes missing.

101
00:06:38,864 --> 00:06:41,503
Okay. Well...

102
00:06:45,043 --> 00:06:46,713
That...

103
00:06:47,682 --> 00:06:49,385
that's incredibly
incriminating.

104
00:06:50,454 --> 00:06:53,125
What about your PI?
Can't he focus on
tracking it down?

105
00:06:53,426 --> 00:06:55,831
I would rather he focus
on tracking down my son.

106
00:06:55,832 --> 00:06:58,403
Sir, I strongly suspect
that Robert knows more

107
00:06:58,670 --> 00:07:00,541
about your son's disappearance
than he's letting on.

108
00:07:00,741 --> 00:07:02,678
If we find it, we find him.

109
00:07:02,679 --> 00:07:04,615
I don't know what to tell you.

110
00:07:06,118 --> 00:07:08,691
Maybe the school has a copy
of the psych report,

111
00:07:08,857 --> 00:07:10,828
you know, in his record, but...

112
00:07:16,305 --> 00:07:20,681
Here, look at this.
My PI found that
under Matt's mattress.

113
00:07:27,361 --> 00:07:29,130
It's a cult.

114
00:07:29,131 --> 00:07:31,903
Matt was in a cult,
and maybe part of me
hopes he still is.

115
00:07:32,270 --> 00:07:35,944
Off on some hippie farm,
smoking God knows what.

116
00:07:41,256 --> 00:07:42,792
I loved him.

117
00:07:46,733 --> 00:07:48,169
I meant, um...

118
00:07:50,407 --> 00:07:51,843
He was my friend.

119
00:07:52,812 --> 00:07:54,181
I loved him. Uh...

120
00:07:55,216 --> 00:07:56,250
love him.

121
00:07:56,251 --> 00:07:57,822
[chuckles]

122
00:07:59,692 --> 00:08:00,828
I do too.

123
00:08:03,132 --> 00:08:04,802
I just hope he knows it.

124
00:08:20,066 --> 00:08:22,605
[sighs]

125
00:08:29,051 --> 00:08:30,186
Send them in.

126
00:08:46,285 --> 00:08:48,255
No smoking, Miss Schaffer.

127
00:08:50,493 --> 00:08:52,798
No, Mrs. Schaffer, you're fine.

128
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:55,069
Miss Schaffer.

129
00:09:01,014 --> 00:09:05,691
Mr. and Mrs. Schaffer,
your daughter is
in a very precarious position,

130
00:09:05,791 --> 00:09:08,864
academically,
behaviorally, and socially.

131
00:09:08,964 --> 00:09:11,903
Her behavior has alienated
classmates and faculty.

132
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:13,873
Now, if I may speak frankly--

133
00:09:13,874 --> 00:09:16,946
Before we get into
the nitty-gritty,
could I get a Tab, please?

134
00:09:17,815 --> 00:09:19,284
- A Tab?
- Hmm.

135
00:09:21,589 --> 00:09:24,929
Lydia, would you please
bring us a Tab?

136
00:09:27,367 --> 00:09:29,973
- What were you saying again?
- Something about Debbie.

137
00:09:30,072 --> 00:09:32,611
As you may know,
it's homecoming week,

138
00:09:32,812 --> 00:09:35,350
and a girl like Debbie
should be a shoo-in
for homecoming court,

139
00:09:35,584 --> 00:09:36,687
but she is not.

140
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:38,923
Instead, she's alienated
half the school.

141
00:09:38,924 --> 00:09:43,633
She's inattentive, unserious,
frequently cruel,

142
00:09:44,067 --> 00:09:46,371
and has contributed virtually
nothing to the community.

143
00:09:46,372 --> 00:09:50,880
Now, may I remind you,
this school is a highly
competitive institution.

144
00:09:50,881 --> 00:09:53,385
She's exhibited patterns
of disruptive behavior,
and we--

145
00:09:53,386 --> 00:09:57,929
Are you kidding me?
Disruptive behavior?
Okay, wow. Wow.

146
00:09:58,028 --> 00:10:01,669
Name one time
I disrupted anyone. One time.

147
00:10:01,670 --> 00:10:03,172
This, right now.

148
00:10:05,611 --> 00:10:09,283
- [Debbie sighs]
- Now, if I may continue.

149
00:10:09,284 --> 00:10:11,689
She skipped her
college application meeting.

150
00:10:11,690 --> 00:10:12,992
Seriously, Debbie?

151
00:10:13,159 --> 00:10:14,996
I had a previous engagement.

152
00:10:15,865 --> 00:10:17,333
<i>I was at the stables.</i>

153
00:10:20,106 --> 00:10:22,645
- You're the only one
that understands me.
- [horse nickers]

154
00:10:24,582 --> 00:10:26,520
<i>Spirit is my best friend,</i>

155
00:10:26,953 --> 00:10:28,289
<i>and, Dr. Croft...</i>

156
00:10:30,059 --> 00:10:32,497
equestrianism
is an extracurricular.

157
00:10:32,665 --> 00:10:34,467
No, it's not. It's a hobby.

158
00:10:34,468 --> 00:10:35,804
[Terry]
A fucking expensive one.

159
00:10:35,805 --> 00:10:38,107
Spirit is a liability
and a money pit.

160
00:10:38,108 --> 00:10:40,346
And if I could pay some thug
to grind his bones up into glue

161
00:10:40,647 --> 00:10:42,450
- without Debbie finding out,
I would.
- Daddy.

162
00:10:42,451 --> 00:10:44,320
Are we done here?
This is feeling overish.

163
00:10:44,321 --> 00:10:46,627
I've got drinks with Pollack
at the Polo Lounge. Sydney.

164
00:10:46,860 --> 00:10:50,901
No, we are not done.
We still need to
talk about the...

165
00:10:52,738 --> 00:10:53,639
The coke.

166
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:54,909
Coke?

167
00:10:55,644 --> 00:10:58,784
As in cocaine? As in drugs?

168
00:10:58,950 --> 00:11:00,888
Yes, drugs. [inhales deeply]

169
00:11:01,088 --> 00:11:05,798
Mr. and Mrs. Schaffer,
at this point,
I have to address the, uh...

170
00:11:05,931 --> 00:11:07,234
[stammers]
the parenting at home...

171
00:11:07,333 --> 00:11:09,303
- Hey, man.
- ...clearly not
servicing your--

172
00:11:09,304 --> 00:11:12,075
- What are you implying?
- [stammers]
I'm not implying anything. I--

173
00:11:12,076 --> 00:11:13,981
Because if you're looking for
a drug user,

174
00:11:14,180 --> 00:11:17,487
I suggest you take a long,
hard look
in whatever reflective surface

175
00:11:17,688 --> 00:11:18,857
you snort off of.

176
00:11:22,430 --> 00:11:26,940
Mrs. Schaffer, Mrs. Schaffer,
I have never ever--

177
00:11:31,048 --> 00:11:32,150
Oh.

178
00:11:32,317 --> 00:11:34,689
Street coke. No surprise there.

179
00:11:35,423 --> 00:11:38,864
I-I refuse to dignify
these allegations
with a denial.

180
00:11:38,997 --> 00:11:41,503
And I refuse
to do your job for you.

181
00:11:42,070 --> 00:11:45,442
Now, my husband and I pay tens
of thousands of dollars
to this school

182
00:11:45,443 --> 00:11:48,684
so that between the hours
of 7:00 a.m. and 3:00 p.m.,

183
00:11:48,817 --> 00:11:50,721
our daughter
is your responsibility.

184
00:11:51,155 --> 00:11:54,094
Either she goes to college,
or you go to jail.

185
00:12:02,845 --> 00:12:05,383
You had my back with Dr. Croft.

186
00:12:06,318 --> 00:12:07,387
You defended me.

187
00:12:07,521 --> 00:12:09,825
Course I did.
You're my daughter.

188
00:12:11,897 --> 00:12:13,933
She's a good mother,
your mother.

189
00:12:15,504 --> 00:12:17,641
[Liz] Debbie, do me a favor.

190
00:12:18,810 --> 00:12:21,447
Find something you do
that looks good for college.

191
00:12:21,448 --> 00:12:24,889
Bluffing and bribery
will only get you
most of the way there.

192
00:12:29,030 --> 00:12:31,135
I just had an amazing idea.

193
00:12:31,468 --> 00:12:32,605
Gotta go.

194
00:12:33,272 --> 00:12:35,042
Thanks for the parenting.

195
00:12:35,811 --> 00:12:37,113
[chuckles softly]

196
00:12:41,789 --> 00:12:43,092
What was that for?

197
00:12:43,192 --> 00:12:46,264
You were electric in there,
Lizzy. [chuckling]

198
00:12:49,237 --> 00:12:50,674
Are you wearing perfume?

199
00:12:51,341 --> 00:12:55,116
It's unisex from Giorgio.
It's very expensive.

200
00:12:55,550 --> 00:12:58,556
- Bret.
- Sorry, meeting with Croft.
Can't talk.

201
00:13:07,407 --> 00:13:09,511
Bret, whatever this is,
it has to wait.

202
00:13:09,512 --> 00:13:11,115
I'm rolling
parent teacher conferences--

203
00:13:11,214 --> 00:13:13,119
Please, Dr. Croft.
Two minutes of your time.

204
00:13:13,285 --> 00:13:14,989
[sighs] What can I do for you?

205
00:13:14,990 --> 00:13:18,329
Ensure my safety
and the safety of every student
at this campus,

206
00:13:18,462 --> 00:13:20,333
especially
during homecoming week.

207
00:13:21,234 --> 00:13:24,876
Robert Mallory has spent time
in a mental institution.

208
00:13:25,076 --> 00:13:26,846
He's on antipsychotics.

209
00:13:26,847 --> 00:13:29,051
I believe that he may be behind
the disappearance
of Matt Kellner,

210
00:13:29,217 --> 00:13:30,285
and the kidnapping of Rhonda.

211
00:13:30,286 --> 00:13:32,223
He killed his own mother.

212
00:13:32,323 --> 00:13:34,293
[Croft] Robert Mallory
killed his mother?

213
00:13:34,294 --> 00:13:35,396
[scoffs]

214
00:13:35,964 --> 00:13:38,870
- Do you have proof?
- There's
a psychiatric evaluation.

215
00:13:39,270 --> 00:13:41,308
I believe it may be
in his file.

216
00:13:42,343 --> 00:13:44,013
I need to see it.

217
00:13:45,584 --> 00:13:47,019
It's not in there, is it?

218
00:13:47,120 --> 00:13:48,957
Look, Bret... [chuckles]
you're a smart kid.

219
00:13:49,090 --> 00:13:51,161
Obviously inventive,
obviously bored.

220
00:13:51,328 --> 00:13:54,534
May I advise you to redirect
that energy into getting
into a good college.

221
00:13:54,535 --> 00:13:54,568
May I advise you to redirect
that energy into getting
into a good college.
Now, if you will excuse me--

222
00:13:54,569 --> 00:13:56,672
Now, if you will excuse me--

223
00:13:56,839 --> 00:13:58,611
The contents of that file are
a matter of campus security.
I need to see it.

224
00:13:58,877 --> 00:14:01,582
[Croft]
What you need, Mr. Ellis,
is to listen.

225
00:14:01,583 --> 00:14:03,052
First of all,

226
00:14:03,152 --> 00:14:04,988
if I knew anything about
any student's mental health,

227
00:14:04,989 --> 00:14:08,262
the last person
I would share it with
would be one of his peers.

228
00:14:08,362 --> 00:14:10,935
The fact that
I'm a student shouldn't matter.

229
00:14:11,068 --> 00:14:12,904
- The reality of the situation--
- Bret,

230
00:14:12,905 --> 00:14:15,678
the reality is
Robert Mallory is a good kid
from a good family.

231
00:14:15,877 --> 00:14:19,885
Only he's in class,
and you're 60 seconds
away from detention.

232
00:14:20,086 --> 00:14:24,394
Fifty-nine, 58, 57.

233
00:14:35,149 --> 00:14:39,625
The theme of the senior class
homecoming float is:

234
00:14:40,459 --> 00:14:46,071
Decadent Restraint
in Conversation with Layers.

235
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:49,277
It's sexy.

236
00:14:50,479 --> 00:14:51,582
Saturated.

237
00:14:52,083 --> 00:14:57,961
- [Debbie chuckles]
- I'm calling it
a tableau bon vivant.

238
00:14:58,462 --> 00:14:59,566
What?

239
00:15:00,232 --> 00:15:02,537
Sorry, what am I looking at?

240
00:15:02,538 --> 00:15:02,571
Sorry, what am I looking at?
[Steven] Ferdinando Scarfiotti.

241
00:15:02,572 --> 00:15:04,374
[Steven] Ferdinando Scarfiotti.

242
00:15:04,474 --> 00:15:06,813
- [Susan mumbles]
- The production designer.

243
00:15:07,514 --> 00:15:11,054
<i>- American Gigolo.</i>
- Yes, yes, but, um...

244
00:15:12,056 --> 00:15:13,692
- are we in this?
- [chuckles]

245
00:15:13,693 --> 00:15:15,095
Like, where do we stand?

246
00:15:15,196 --> 00:15:17,199
I don't know, Susan,
where do you stand?

247
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:19,505
Where do you stand
on the merits, nay,

248
00:15:19,638 --> 00:15:23,411
moral necessity
of artistic expression?

249
00:15:23,412 --> 00:15:26,919
Steven,
I can tell how much thought
you've put into this.

250
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:28,656
- What?
- It's very conceptual.

251
00:15:29,357 --> 00:15:30,360
It's very clever.

252
00:15:30,527 --> 00:15:32,029
- Thank you.
- No, it's not.

253
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:34,634
I love it. I do. I just, um...

254
00:15:34,635 --> 00:15:35,603
[Steven] Spit it out.

255
00:15:35,604 --> 00:15:37,941
[Susan] It's almost too...

256
00:15:40,514 --> 00:15:45,021
It's... too... perfect.

257
00:15:45,022 --> 00:15:46,925
Oh. [chuckles softly]

258
00:15:46,926 --> 00:15:50,968
Yeah,
like me in my perfect dress,

259
00:15:51,201 --> 00:15:55,977
with my hair perfectly set
after hours
of curling iron torture,

260
00:15:55,978 --> 00:16:01,756
waving perfectly
on this perfectly
designed float.

261
00:16:02,624 --> 00:16:05,496
It's just a lot for me.

262
00:16:06,198 --> 00:16:09,003
A lot to constantly maintain.

263
00:16:09,705 --> 00:16:11,074
[sighs]

264
00:16:12,744 --> 00:16:15,382
You know,
sometimes I think to myself,

265
00:16:16,586 --> 00:16:18,021
"Susan,

266
00:16:19,658 --> 00:16:21,996
just do something bad.

267
00:16:23,298 --> 00:16:28,041
Do something unexpected.

268
00:16:31,014 --> 00:16:32,684
Do something...

269
00:16:34,555 --> 00:16:36,057
dangerous."

270
00:16:36,592 --> 00:16:38,160
I'm just so tired

271
00:16:38,161 --> 00:16:43,304
of being
Miss Goody-goody Nice Girl
Queen of Everything

272
00:16:43,305 --> 00:16:44,876
all the time, you know?

273
00:16:45,510 --> 00:16:47,581
[Steven] So what do you want?

274
00:16:48,683 --> 00:16:50,554
With the float.

275
00:16:53,325 --> 00:16:54,528
I don't know.

276
00:16:54,929 --> 00:16:56,431
[inhales deeply]

277
00:17:01,074 --> 00:17:02,611
Maybe something...

278
00:17:05,083 --> 00:17:06,752
purposefully...

279
00:17:08,455 --> 00:17:09,592
terrible.

280
00:17:10,393 --> 00:17:11,227
[scoffs]

281
00:17:11,228 --> 00:17:12,731
No, like, okay.

282
00:17:13,165 --> 00:17:17,742
Me, standing up there,
you know,

283
00:17:18,710 --> 00:17:21,715
and it's--
and it's r-really bad, like...

284
00:17:21,716 --> 00:17:25,189
Like freshman
amateur hour level bad.

285
00:17:25,891 --> 00:17:29,966
People would freak out.
They would lose their minds.

286
00:17:30,634 --> 00:17:34,174
And it-- it means
I would have made a mistake,

287
00:17:34,642 --> 00:17:36,513
for once in my life.

288
00:17:37,380 --> 00:17:43,458
You can't imagine how freeing
that would feel for me
to lose for once.

289
00:17:44,596 --> 00:17:46,063
[inhales deeply]

290
00:17:46,064 --> 00:17:50,707
I'm just supposed to be happy
and grateful all the time.

291
00:17:53,245 --> 00:17:54,380
Like I said...

292
00:17:56,586 --> 00:17:57,788
it's a lot.

293
00:18:02,397 --> 00:18:05,169
- Okay, doll. I get it.
- [Susan chuckles]

294
00:18:05,369 --> 00:18:09,310
It's a lot of expectation
to always be perfect,
never make a mistake.

295
00:18:09,311 --> 00:18:11,715
Trust me. I know.

296
00:18:11,716 --> 00:18:13,085
Okay, Steven...

297
00:18:13,352 --> 00:18:17,961
But my relentlessly demanding
boss, Mr. Terry Schaffer,

298
00:18:17,962 --> 00:18:22,672
has instructed me
to go all out with my talents.

299
00:18:23,305 --> 00:18:26,212
Because he wants his selfish,
ungrateful daughter
Debbie here,

300
00:18:26,344 --> 00:18:30,553
who is suddenly and shockingly
on the senior class homecoming
float committee, to--

301
00:18:30,654 --> 00:18:32,123
It's for my school record,
I have told--

302
00:18:32,256 --> 00:18:33,592
Mr. Schaffer
wants the senior class

303
00:18:33,593 --> 00:18:35,930
to win this best float thing,

304
00:18:36,131 --> 00:18:41,908
so a Ferdinando Scarfiotti
tribute is how this float
is gonna go down.

305
00:18:41,909 --> 00:18:41,941
so a Ferdinando Scarfiotti
tribute is how this float
is gonna go down.
I have made myself clear.

306
00:18:41,942 --> 00:18:43,680
I have made myself clear.

307
00:18:43,780 --> 00:18:45,784
Steven, enough with
the histrionics, please.

308
00:18:45,917 --> 00:18:48,388
You're like a thousand
years old.

309
00:18:48,589 --> 00:18:52,397
You have no idea
about the pressures that girls
like Susan and I face.

310
00:18:52,564 --> 00:18:55,771
Let me leave you with this:

311
00:18:57,340 --> 00:19:02,216
I am more than an assistant
and much more than your fucking
drug mule, Miss Debbie.

312
00:19:02,684 --> 00:19:06,893
I am an artist.
I am a professional.

313
00:19:07,661 --> 00:19:11,736
I have something to offer
and something to say.

314
00:19:14,273 --> 00:19:16,010
And one day, one day very soon,

315
00:19:16,011 --> 00:19:19,551
people are going to be forced
to listen to me.

316
00:19:20,553 --> 00:19:22,624
Whether they like it or not.

317
00:19:24,528 --> 00:19:27,768
Are you wearing perfume?
[chuckles]

318
00:19:28,603 --> 00:19:30,206
Giorgio.

319
00:19:30,406 --> 00:19:33,746
You smell like an old woman
trying to smell
like a little girl.

320
00:19:33,846 --> 00:19:37,020
Okay, do me a favor, babe.
Envy me less.

321
00:19:37,286 --> 00:19:41,194
And work harder
on that tire of flab
around your gut.

322
00:19:41,195 --> 00:19:43,365
Oh. That's mean.

323
00:19:45,870 --> 00:19:48,976
- I deserved it.
- [Steven] Yes, you did,
Little Debbie.

324
00:19:53,719 --> 00:19:55,689
Look, about the float--

325
00:19:55,690 --> 00:19:56,692
Honey, I get it.

326
00:19:57,661 --> 00:20:01,869
I understand how hard it is
for you to win
and how people hate you for it.

327
00:20:02,270 --> 00:20:03,573
People hate me?

328
00:20:04,542 --> 00:20:05,644
I...

329
00:20:06,579 --> 00:20:09,250
I know you don't want
to be homecoming queen either.

330
00:20:09,251 --> 00:20:10,520
I really don't.

331
00:20:12,256 --> 00:20:13,526
Great.

332
00:20:14,360 --> 00:20:16,298
I have an amazing idea.

333
00:20:16,431 --> 00:20:18,101
I think
you're gonna flip for it,

334
00:20:18,335 --> 00:20:22,275
and based on
your breakdown back there,
my timing is impeccable.

335
00:20:22,276 --> 00:20:25,349
- Okay, follow me.
- What, wait,
where are we going?

336
00:20:25,517 --> 00:20:27,320
To your prearranged salvation.

337
00:20:27,486 --> 00:20:29,691
- Come on. [chuckles]
- Okay.

338
00:20:32,732 --> 00:20:34,167
Oh, hey, Rhonda.

339
00:20:34,433 --> 00:20:36,806
- We're meeting Rhonda.
Hope that's okay.
- Of course.

340
00:20:36,939 --> 00:20:39,177
- Hey, Rhonda.
- Okay, good news.

341
00:20:39,343 --> 00:20:41,849
I've decided, and Susan agrees,

342
00:20:42,383 --> 00:20:43,886
to make you homecoming queen.

343
00:20:43,887 --> 00:20:45,222
- What?
- What?

344
00:20:45,223 --> 00:20:47,125
[Debbie] Yeah, Susan will be
the front runner, right?

345
00:20:47,126 --> 00:20:50,099
But since she agrees with me
that it really should be you,

346
00:20:50,332 --> 00:20:52,937
she's gonna
tell all of her supporters
to vote for you.

347
00:20:52,938 --> 00:20:54,774
And so, at homecoming,

348
00:20:54,775 --> 00:20:58,414
it's gonna be you up there
on that winner's stand,
crown in place.

349
00:20:58,415 --> 00:21:00,686
Debbie,
that's actually a great idea.

350
00:21:00,687 --> 00:21:03,660
Sidebar later
on the word "actually."
[scoffs]

351
00:21:03,826 --> 00:21:05,564
Rhonda, you totally deserve it.

352
00:21:05,763 --> 00:21:08,602
I'd be honored to vote for you,
and I bet everybody else
would be, too.

353
00:21:08,603 --> 00:21:10,172
Totally.

354
00:21:10,305 --> 00:21:13,477
And if you both
want to tell everyone,

355
00:21:13,478 --> 00:21:15,016
including Principal Croft,

356
00:21:15,282 --> 00:21:19,456
that this charitable endeavor
was entirely my idea,

357
00:21:19,457 --> 00:21:23,431
and actually it's becoming
an official
scholastic committee,

358
00:21:23,432 --> 00:21:24,802
everybody wins.

359
00:21:25,302 --> 00:21:27,339
Except Susan, of course.

360
00:21:29,945 --> 00:21:31,849
But I'm not beautiful though.

361
00:21:31,949 --> 00:21:33,218
- It doesn't matter.
- Of course you are.

362
00:21:33,485 --> 00:21:34,588
Debbie.

363
00:21:39,998 --> 00:21:41,635
I don't know what to say.

364
00:21:46,444 --> 00:21:48,415
"Thank you, Debbie Schaffer."

365
00:21:50,820 --> 00:21:51,956
Yeah.

366
00:22:03,546 --> 00:22:05,750
[electricity crackles]

367
00:22:13,131 --> 00:22:15,503
[police radio chatter]

368
00:22:30,266 --> 00:22:32,537
[indistinct chatter]

369
00:22:44,227 --> 00:22:45,730
[Donaldson] Jesus Christ.

370
00:22:46,899 --> 00:22:49,035
[Fregosi chuckles] Still think
it's the Night Stalker?

371
00:22:49,036 --> 00:22:50,773
It's not inconsistent.

372
00:22:51,875 --> 00:22:54,113
The mutilations,
the sexual sadism.

373
00:22:54,114 --> 00:22:57,185
Jim, come on,
there's no way this
is the Night Stalker.

374
00:22:57,186 --> 00:23:01,094
This is not the same guy who's
bludgeoning little old ladies
in their beds.

375
00:23:02,931 --> 00:23:04,702
This is The Trawler.

376
00:23:04,901 --> 00:23:08,241
If we go on TV and confirm that
there's multiple serial killers
on the loose...

377
00:23:09,043 --> 00:23:13,118
Bedlam in the streets
of Los Angeles,
and we get shitcanned.

378
00:23:15,055 --> 00:23:18,295
The young victim, Seth Tracy,
was just 18 years old,

379
00:23:18,529 --> 00:23:22,168
an honor student at Crossroads
bound for Princeton next fall.

380
00:23:22,169 --> 00:23:25,041
<i>Police strongly suspect
this could be the work</i>

381
00:23:25,042 --> 00:23:27,346
<i>of a serial slayer known
as the Night Stalker.</i>

382
00:23:27,814 --> 00:23:31,989
<i>However, a source inside
the department told us
about a secondary theory.</i>

383
00:23:32,089 --> 00:23:33,992
<i>This brutal killing
could be the work</i>

384
00:23:33,993 --> 00:23:36,196
<i>of an increasingly
dangerous individual</i>

385
00:23:36,197 --> 00:23:36,229
<i>of an increasingly
dangerous individual
police are calling The Trawler,</i>

386
00:23:36,230 --> 00:23:38,367
<i>police are calling The Trawler,</i>

387
00:23:38,368 --> 00:23:42,843
<i>a reference to
a disturbing pattern
of mutilation involving fish.</i>

388
00:23:42,844 --> 00:23:44,914
[Bret] <i>The time between murders
was decreasing.</i>

389
00:23:44,915 --> 00:23:48,888
<i>It was almost as if the killer
was gaining confidence,
a strength.</i>

390
00:23:48,889 --> 00:23:51,963
<i>I, too, felt newly emboldened.
I had to stop him.</i>

391
00:23:52,564 --> 00:23:56,839
<i>This time
I didn't bother with Croft.
I went straight to the police.</i>

392
00:24:01,982 --> 00:24:04,153
Bret Ellis,
crime reporter, <i>LA Times.</i>

393
00:24:04,487 --> 00:24:06,425
I have some information
about The Trawler case.

394
00:24:06,592 --> 00:24:08,461
I'd like to see the case files.

395
00:24:09,163 --> 00:24:10,667
Freedom of Information Act.

396
00:24:11,835 --> 00:24:16,846
[Bret] <i>Debbie's fake ID guy
was expensive.
And worth every penny.</i>

397
00:24:18,015 --> 00:24:21,020
Wait here.
Detectives will come
and get you.

398
00:24:21,722 --> 00:24:23,793
Hope you help them
get the fucker.

399
00:25:29,791 --> 00:25:30,893
Okay.

400
00:25:31,862 --> 00:25:32,997
Who the hell are you?

401
00:25:34,133 --> 00:25:36,471
I should throw your ass in jail
for trespassing.

402
00:25:36,638 --> 00:25:38,709
Fine, but hear me out first.

403
00:25:42,550 --> 00:25:45,256
You guys are way off base
with the Night Stalker.

404
00:25:46,058 --> 00:25:47,893
This is The Trawler,
and you know it,

405
00:25:47,894 --> 00:25:50,767
and whoever The Trawler is,
he's a member of the community.

406
00:25:50,867 --> 00:25:53,606
Oh. Well, that really
narrows it down. [chuckles]

407
00:25:53,739 --> 00:25:55,544
Think about it.
Why would a serial killer

408
00:25:55,777 --> 00:25:59,316
who strangles people
in their homes
deface our school mascot?

409
00:26:01,154 --> 00:26:03,124
And you have
information about this.

410
00:26:04,961 --> 00:26:06,130
No.

411
00:26:08,234 --> 00:26:09,304
Yes.

412
00:26:09,705 --> 00:26:11,842
I was there.
I saw it happen. It...

413
00:26:12,744 --> 00:26:14,781
It was a coincidence, okay?

414
00:26:19,256 --> 00:26:21,393
- What about Cha Cha Rodriguez?
- [scoffs]

415
00:26:21,394 --> 00:26:24,934
Teenage girl brutalized
and killed in the bathroom
during a live TV taping.

416
00:26:24,935 --> 00:26:27,373
Does that really sound
like the Night Stalker to you?

417
00:26:27,674 --> 00:26:29,343
Sneaking onto a studio lot
in broad daylight?

418
00:26:29,611 --> 00:26:32,382
That-- No, no, we got someone
that looks good for that.

419
00:26:32,383 --> 00:26:33,752
A lover's spat.

420
00:26:39,731 --> 00:26:41,067
Are you joking?

421
00:26:42,403 --> 00:26:43,573
It's another kid.

422
00:26:44,240 --> 00:26:46,211
Someone who's desperate
for attention.

423
00:26:46,645 --> 00:26:49,718
He's dumping bodies
on tennis courts,
playgrounds, dance floors,

424
00:26:49,885 --> 00:26:51,453
places rich kids play games

425
00:26:51,454 --> 00:26:54,460
because this is
also a game to him,
and he's winning.

426
00:26:55,295 --> 00:26:58,569
These are deeply personalized
fetishistic killings.

427
00:26:59,370 --> 00:27:02,810
He's mutilating his peers
because of how
we make him feel.

428
00:27:03,445 --> 00:27:04,714
Like a freak.

429
00:27:05,449 --> 00:27:07,318
Think about Manson.

430
00:27:07,319 --> 00:27:10,324
His victims were people
or the sort of people
who made him feel...

431
00:27:10,325 --> 00:27:12,596
[takes deep breath]

432
00:27:12,597 --> 00:27:14,467
...rejected socially.

433
00:27:15,168 --> 00:27:17,239
I mean,
Didion covers all of this.

434
00:27:21,014 --> 00:27:22,482
In her book, <i>The White Album?</i>

435
00:27:22,483 --> 00:27:25,188
This has been helpful.
Thanks for coming in.

436
00:27:28,161 --> 00:27:29,230
Look.

437
00:27:30,433 --> 00:27:34,172
The suspect
you should be looking at
is a kid named Robert Mallory.

438
00:27:34,173 --> 00:27:36,813
He's a dangerous psychopath
who left his school in Chicago

439
00:27:36,979 --> 00:27:38,817
because
he was institutionalized.

440
00:27:39,316 --> 00:27:41,419
And he definitely
killed his mom.

441
00:27:41,420 --> 00:27:41,453
And he definitely
killed his mom.
And probably Matt Kellner.

442
00:27:41,454 --> 00:27:43,291
And probably Matt Kellner.

443
00:27:50,038 --> 00:27:51,441
It's the strangest thing.

444
00:27:52,710 --> 00:27:56,117
I can't tell if he knows
a lot more or a lot less
than he's letting on.

445
00:27:57,085 --> 00:27:59,691
Rich kids for you. [scoffs]

446
00:28:00,392 --> 00:28:01,728
Let's tail him.

447
00:28:02,296 --> 00:28:04,701
[indistinct chatter]

448
00:28:27,880 --> 00:28:29,751
What are we looking for, Thom?

449
00:28:30,485 --> 00:28:31,822
I feel weird.

450
00:28:32,857 --> 00:28:34,059
People can see us.

451
00:28:35,830 --> 00:28:38,067
We should do this later,
not at school.

452
00:28:39,872 --> 00:28:41,942
You still care
what people think.

453
00:28:42,744 --> 00:28:43,846
Don't you?

454
00:28:45,049 --> 00:28:50,826
Your friends
are inconsequential
to your enlightenment journey.

455
00:28:52,129 --> 00:28:53,131
Susan...

456
00:28:53,465 --> 00:28:57,039
- Susan is nothing.
- Susan's the love of my life.

457
00:28:58,341 --> 00:29:03,552
Her presence is neither
additive nor detrimental.

458
00:29:03,786 --> 00:29:04,921
I just...

459
00:29:06,692 --> 00:29:10,232
I don't know,
I feel like everything
in my life is falling apart.

460
00:29:10,833 --> 00:29:13,071
That is exactly
where I want you to be.

461
00:29:15,208 --> 00:29:20,284
It's in the loss of identity
that we find
authentic spiritual purpose.

462
00:29:20,285 --> 00:29:22,022
I'm so lost.

463
00:29:24,928 --> 00:29:26,497
Things used to make sense.

464
00:29:29,871 --> 00:29:31,808
- I miss my dad.
- Hmm.

465
00:29:33,044 --> 00:29:35,049
The way he was
when I was young.

466
00:29:35,215 --> 00:29:37,118
Count the hours
till he came home

467
00:29:37,119 --> 00:29:41,293
so I could ask him
to put me in a blanket
and swing me around the yard.

468
00:29:46,203 --> 00:29:48,041
And one day he came home and...

469
00:29:50,079 --> 00:29:51,414
he wasn't smiling.

470
00:29:54,988 --> 00:29:56,525
Then everything changed.

471
00:29:58,494 --> 00:30:02,904
And he started telling me
that I was just like my mom.

472
00:30:07,814 --> 00:30:10,051
And then he finally ran off
with some...

473
00:30:11,955 --> 00:30:17,065
fucking slut cocktail waitress.

474
00:30:23,612 --> 00:30:24,948
I'm so broken.

475
00:30:26,217 --> 00:30:28,154
I just want my dad. [cries]

476
00:30:29,357 --> 00:30:31,127
[Bruce shushes]

477
00:30:31,394 --> 00:30:32,897
Look at me, Thom.

478
00:30:34,868 --> 00:30:37,406
The man
who was once your father,

479
00:30:38,108 --> 00:30:41,280
he took your lantern,
and he departed.

480
00:30:42,082 --> 00:30:43,686
You still crave guidance.

481
00:30:43,852 --> 00:30:45,355
You don't need your father.

482
00:30:45,856 --> 00:30:47,493
You don't need Susan.

483
00:30:47,994 --> 00:30:50,566
I have your lantern.
I will guide you.

484
